This "sometimes get through" and a nature of an error you are quoting
seems to suggest that you have problems with reading your distribution
media whatever they may be (CDs?, hard disk?, over a network?) and that
would indicate possible hardware problem.  Is that excluded?

In any case, regardless of possible anaconda bugs,  if you managed
"get through" at least once with any kind of an installation then
you can easily add and remove packages to your installation, and
customize it the way you desire, either by using directly 'rpm'
from a command line or via 'gnorpm' graphic interface or even
using 'nautilus' for that purpose (if you are patient enough).
You do not have to install it in a "final form" and sometimes this
is not even possible; some things are getting installed always
and this is not a truly minimal required set.

