I have had no trouble NFS exporting RAID 1 or RAID 5 sets from Linux
systems, Alpha or P4.  However, I would like to voice some thoughts
on software RAID.

I think RAID 5 is more efficient than RAID 1 as far as disk usage for
protecting data, but RAID 1 will be much more efficient as far as CPU
usage; implementing RAID 5 in software tends to use a lot of CPU while
RAID 1 requires very little CPU.  My previous experience was with
Veritas on Sun/SPARC systems:  A RAID 5 using a Sun A5000 FC array ran
about as fast as a single fast SCSI drive, while sucking up a lot of
CPU time for any file transaction.  The only thing it bought me was the
parity protection (and the Veritas logical volume manager allowed me to
dynamically grow or shrink a volume).  I know that Alphas and P4s are
much faster than SPARCs, but in general, calculating all the parity
data will take up a lot of CPU cycles, varying with the level of NFS
activity, which can already present quite a bit of overhead.

For this reason, I still perfer hardware based RAID systems.  In
particular, I have recently acquired a 3ware RAID card that uses ATA
drives.  The cost of the card and four 200GB drives was actually about
the same as ONE 140GB SCSI drive.  Unfortunately, they do not have
drivers for Linux/Alpha.  But this appears to be a very popular option
for Linux based NFS servers.  3ware has a card that handles 16 drives,
so in a RAID 5 configuration, you use 1 as parity and 15 for data,
which gives you about 3 TB capacity on one card using 200 GB drives.  A
typical P4/2GHz system can make use of 2 such RAID sets before reaching
bus saturation.  So you can get a 6 TB NFS server in about 8 rack units,
for about $35,000, depending on your vendor.

eyc

