On Tue, Jan 07, 2003 at 11:20:33AM +0100, Janez Gradisek wrote:

> I tried installing from CD but it didn't work. I used
> 
> >>>boot dqb1 -fi kernels/vmlinux.gz -fl "root=/dev/hdb initrd=images/cdrom.img"

This is actually a wrong command line.  Assuming that 'dqb1' is
indeed your CD (does 'show dev' from SRM indeed indicates that?)
you should use

   boot dqb1 -fl 0

or 

   boot dqb1 -fl i

if you want to pick up interactively by label one of remaining
predefined options.  "-fl 0" is somewhat close to what you were using
but not quite.  "root=/dev/hdb" is surely something you do not want
to see there.

> and got the following error messages:
> 
> ...
> aboot: booting from device 'IDE 0 11 1 1 1 0 0'
> 
> cb_open: failed IDE 0 11 1 1 1 0 0, dqb1.1.1.11.0
> aboot: unable to open boot device 'IDE ...0' c0...0

Which makes me wonder if your CD is really dqb1.  If yes then maybe you
have a very old firmware with troubles?

> 
> With 2 floppies I use:
> 
> >>>boot dva0 -fi vmlinux.gz -fl "root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1" 

Which, once again, is not what you really want.  Try "b dva0 -fl 0"
or "b dva0 -fl i" instead.

> and it goes well until either disk formating (just before installation of 
> packages) or network configuration (when clicking Next after entering the 
> IPs), when anaconda reports a bug.

Maybe anaconda could be more robust but its expected work environment is
_not_ what you supply.

Sometimes it is possible to find more detailed information about
errors and their causes looking at text consoles 1, 2, 3 or 4.  There
could be also some log files in /tmp on your ramdisk.

  Michal




