With regards to your message at 08:18 PM 1/7/03, Thursday AKA dr._john. 
Where you stated:
>To the list:
>
>I got a line on a UP1500 mobo and processor at a decent price. The guy
>is in biz and will give me a 30-day warranty.  Should I buy it?  Is
>there anything i should know, like it only works with one video card or
>will only run on Dead Cat Linux?
The boards are fairly compatible , as long as you have the more recent 
version of SRM firmware on them.
In general they are pretty forgiving about video cards, etc.
The reason there is only limited warranty available is that Samsung stopped 
making Alpha boards about a year ago, and sold their last ones to the 
vendors early last year, and only provided one year warranty on them.
If one fails, there are no replacements to be had by the vendors.
For example, our firm sold quite a few of them, kept some spares for 
warranty, and now as the warranties are drawing to a close are selling off 
the ones left.

>Seriously, what would be a good configuration for SRM, SCSI card, video
>& sound cards, NIC, and power supply?  The seller said it uses DDR ram
>... is this a good thing?

SRM version I can provide if needed.
SCSI : Works wit pretty well anything, but if you want to boot, SRM must 
recognize the card.
Symbios Logic/LSI cards up to U160 levels work. So do most modern Adaptec.
Video, it can work nicely with Radeon, TNT, some GeForce, and Oxygen cards.
It has onboard Tulip NIC.
DDR is like SDRAM, but at effective doubled data rates. It is the modern 
standard for most boards.



With our best regards,

