On Wednesday, January 8, 2003, at 12:37  AM, axp@sprawl.net wrote:
>   2) I can't believe HP is burying the Alpha. Even if some of the EV8
>     features will be integrated into Itanium. Just seems like a waste
>     to me.

Actually, Compaq buried Alpha before the HP deal.

But the most interesting aspects of things is that contrary to the talk 
of a year ago, when Compaq sold the Alpha team to Intel; Itanium will 
NOT be the HP/Intel design with a couple of EV8 features thrown in, but 
more like an EV8 that includes the Intel instruction set. I get the 
feeling that more and more of the original HP/Intel design is being 
thrown out as they try to get Itanium performance up to the EV7 level.

The implications behind that change are enormous -- it potentially 
means that Itanium 2005 really will be what EV8 was supposed to have 
been. There are also features of the original Intel/HP design which are 
potentially VERY powerful and useful, things like the bi-endian nature 
-- big/little endian, (Possibly at the executing instruction level!) 
The second feature actually was part of EV8 -- lockstep instructions 
designed for Tandem.

Remember the VAX... it could run PDP code for the first couple of 
iterations
because of imbedded PDP instructions. In the PDP to Vax to Alpha 
transition, DEC pioneered some pretty serious emulation and binary 
translation technologies. Technologies further honed by the industry in 
the Apple PowerPC transition. Once you get the compiler technologies in 
place, which also went to Intel, the kinds of things that HP and Intel 
did in silicon because they didn't have those compiler technologies can 
easily be replaced, or possibly even fixed. (Remember, Tru64 has run on 
Itanium since before the first silicon - the Itanium emulator ran on 
the Alpha - and VMS has since September 2001.) The new IBM 970 PowerPC 
chip, based on their Power 4 runs both 64 and 32 bit code - and at 
1.8ghz/42watts or 1.2ghz/19watts they don't have the heat problem which 
has plagued both Intel and HP in their chip designs to date.

Apple has just upped the anti in the PC world -- The new 'iLife" tools 
are going to be CPU and storage eaters! As the WinTel world follows, 32 
bit systems are going to become seriously constrained. (Don't you want 
your "home computer" to be your TvIO, music center, answering machine, 
security monitor, HVAC controller ...?)

There has always been serious potential in the Alpha/Intel deal; it's 
finally beginning to look like Alpha may have lost a battle, but won 
the war.

T.T.F.N.
William H. Magill
