>> A new problem:
>> Now I can not mount the CD-RW device. The corresponding line in fstab is:
>> /dev/cdrom         /mnt/cdrom        iso9660 noauto,owner,kudzu,ro 0 0

> And /dev/cdrom is a link to what?  
It was linked to /dev/hdd.

>> Looking at the loaded modules using lsmod I find no ide-scsi module, so I 
>> loaded one with modprobe ide-scsi. It still doesn't work.

> 'ide-scsi' module may be needed if you want to _write_ on your CD-RW.
> If you are trying only to read then accessing it as an ATAPI CD should
> be enough.  It happens that devices misidentify themselves and/or are
> misdetected.  In such situation your CD will likely be taken for an IDE
> floppy and you can see that in your logs.  You may tell Linux that this
> is really CD. Assuming that this is "hdb" add "hdb=cdrom" to your boot
> commands defined in /etc/aboot.conf.  If you want to use a SCSI
> emulation then use "hdb=scsi" and 'ide-scsi' should be loaded on a
> startup.  Change "hdb" to what you really have.
This was the right way to go. I added hdd=ide-scsi to aboot.conf and dmesg 
said:
...
Attached scsi CD-ROM sr0 at scsi1, channel 0, id 0, lun 0
sr0: scsi3-mmc drive: 40x/40x writer cd/rw xa/form2 cdda tray
...
that CD-ROM was attached to /dev/sr0, but this device was not there. So I made 
it and the things are working fine now.

Thank you all for your help,

Janez




