I haven't been able to find "real" definitions of either of these two 
terms.

Do you know if the UUID is the same as the Worldwide Name aka WWID 
(World Wide ID)?

According to Compaq (Tru64 Unix context):
===============
"A worldwide name is a unique number assigned to a subsystem by the 
Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE) and set by the 
manufacturer prior to shipping. The worldwide name assigned to a 
subsystem never changes. We recommend that you obtain and record the 
worldwide names of Fibre Channel components in case you need to verify 
their target ID mappings in the operating system.

Fibre Channel devices have both a node name and a port name worldwide 
name, both of which are
64-bit numbers.
===============
Compaq has no reference to UUID that I can find.

> Disk dev names are still dynamically assigned, but in fstabs you can 
> use the volume id (UUID) to specify the device you're mounting

The UUID is apparently used in the *BSD automount arena, where the 
above comment was made.

T.T.F.N.
