On Tuesday 21 January 2003 01:28 am, Tim Pugh wrote:
> I'm looking for experiences from people using RH7.2 and IDE
> drives on a Digital Personal Workstation 500a.  My own
> experiences have been mixed.

Well, my own experience with the 500a is all secondhand, but I 
can offer a few ideas.

<snip account of laborious troubleshooting>
> I believe the IDE controller chips are working because the IDE
> disk worked in RH7.0, but not in RH7.2.  The journal
> filesystem appears to work fine except when it's getting bad
> I/O transfer results.  I believe the problem is in the kernel
> driver for the IDE controller (maybe due to flaky hardware,
> but I'm not convinced.)  As a test,the IDE disk and CDROM were
> configured as master/slave on a single channel, then
> configured as single devices on seperate IDE channels.  Same
> problems occurred under both configurations.
>
> Conclusion, don't use IDE disk drives with RH7.2 alpha on a
> DPW500a. Would an PCI EIDE controller board fix the problem? 
> Maybe...would it use different IDE drivers?  Any experiences
> before I try it?

Well, I wouldn't go getting extra hardware if the software seems 
to be the problem--even if extra hardware might step around it.  
First thing I'd do is compile a "stock" Linux kernel and see if 
it has the same problem.  If that fixes your case, well, you've 
got a good workaround, and you can submit a RedHat bug report 
(and your workaround) at your leisure.

If that doesn't work, you could try setting the drive to transfer 
in PIO mode.  This will be a lot slower but potentially more 
stable.

Also, I've heard of people installing Promise UDMA133 controllers 
in the PWS 500a; Linux seems to handle it just fine, although 
booting from it is obviously a no-go.  If you decide to go the 
new hardware route, the core IDE layer will be the same, but the 
hardware-specific part of the IDE driver will be different--so 
it may or may not step around the problem.  On the bright side, 
if it all doesn't work out and you have to go back to 7.0, 
you'll at least have a much faster IDE controller.

-- 
Kelledin
