That's a messy looking error report.  I'm nowhere near an
expert, but here are a couple of ideas until the experts
reply:

If this is indeed at the end of the install process, so that
everything else needed for a complete install has finished,
you might be able to run swriteboot manually.  The argv
message above gives what looks like the complete command
line.

Do you have a way of booting the machine?  My strategy is
to devote a partition on each physical disk drive for a
test or fallback installation.  This gives me a total of
three OS installations on the machine, so if one goes sour
I can always boot from one of the other two.  If you don't
have such a setup, it should be feasible to boot from the
installation CDs, hit ALT-CTRL-F2 to get to a root shell
prompt, and manually run swriteboot from there.  The device
node might be somewhere other than /dev, perhaps under /tmp.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
