On Fri, Jan 24, 2003 at 11:34:44AM +0000, Nelson Brito wrote:
> > Yes, 
> > I change now e put just one disk /dev/sda and be continued whith error...
> > 
> > I press ALT-F2 e don't find the command swriteboot ? 
> 
> 
> I'm sure that Michal Jaegermann
> does not need my support on this, but what
> he said is what might be happen with you...
> I'm sure that he is absulletly sure on what he's
> saing :-)

Nelson, you have more faith in me than I have myself. :-)

Still, swriteboot should be somewhere.  If not in your current PATH (I
truly do not remember all minute details of a layout during an
installation) then on a system you are installing right now. 'mount'
will tell you where this is mounted and 'swriteboot' should be there in
'sbin'.  If not then an installation of 'aboot' package failed, as it
was suggested before, and you should find why.  The most important is
checking if partitioning is correct.

> Or you can try to do it manually botting the
> machine with a floppy or from cd and write
> aboot with swriteboot... if you have a bsd disk
> label of course!!!

This is also a good suggestion.  Then 'swriteboot' should be
in a root PATH and if not then you can try again to install missing
pieces.  Boot into a single user mode - for various reasons.

Assuming that your root file system  is on /dev/sda2 booting from
a floppy made from images/generic.img will look like that

  b dva0 -fi vmlinux.gz -fl "load_ramdisk=1 root=/dev/sda2 single"

although booting from CD with '-fl 4' (rescue) may work even better.


  Michal




