When the CCC and CXX compilers are installed, they probe the system to find crtbegin.o and then create a link to it. It is possible the installation procedure got confused since your system has more then one of them. The easiest way to work around this is create a link from /usr/lib/compaq/cxx-6.5.9.31/alpha-linux/bin/crtbegin.o to /usr/lib/gcc-lib/alpha-redhat-linux/3.0.4/crtbegin.o.

Just out of curiosity, what is /usr/lib/compaq/cxx-6.5.9.31/alpha-linux/bin/crtbegin.o currently pointing to?

-----Original Message-----
From: Jiann-Ming Su [mailto:js290@bellsouth.net]
Sent: Sunday, January 26, 2003 12:11 PM
To: axp-list@redhat.com
Subject: broken links in cxx/ccc?

I just installed ccc-6.5.9.001-6 and cxx-6.5.9.31-1, and some files seem
to be missing.

$ cxx /?
ld: cannot open /usr/lib/compaq/cxx-6.5.9.31/alpha-linux/bin/crtbegin.o: No such file or directory
ld: cannot open /usr/lib/compaq/cxx-6.5.9.31/alpha-linux/bin/crtbegin.o: No such file or directory

Looks like crtbegin.o is a link to /usr/lib/crtbegin.o, which doesn't appear
to exist.  I do have gcc versions:

$ locate crtbegin.o
/usr/lib/gcc-lib/alpha-redhat-linux/2.96/crtbegin.o
/usr/lib/gcc-lib/alpha-redhat-linux/3.0.4/crtbegin.o
/usr/alpha-glibc21-linux/lib/crtbegin.o

What am I missing?  Thanks for any info.

