no, the gs320 is not a marvel system but he asked for information on the 
alphas. those white papers are all the ones that are available unless you ask 
your local HP contact. 

just a quick rundown on how the marvel talks to it's memory (and in no way is 
it comprehensive). 

each "dynamic duo" board has two cpus. each cpu has two zboxes (0 & 1). each 
zbox has a bank of RIMMs, consisiting of 4 RIMM slots for actual memory and a 
5th slot for parity. the parity is an optional thing, but if a RIMM is 
installed, it must be of the same size as the others in the board (in other 
words, if you have 2GB of memory in 512MB RIMMs, the parity RIMM has to be 
512MB). 
since each cpu has the zbox locally, if any of the cpus have to access memory 
that belongs to a remote processor, the request gets routed through the NWSE 
ports (if it's a gs1280 Mx) or through NS if it's an ES47/80. the lag time is 
minimal, about 300ms from the middle of the torus mesh to the exteriors. 

Cheers, 

Rodolfo

