PS:

Someone can correct me if my observations are wrong, but I see a very
interesting approach taken in the implementation of the newer Alpha
processors:  A lot of the functions that we traditionally see on the OS
level have been put into silicon.  Things like treating memory
interconnects like networks, and intelligence to route data
efficiently, (and the SMT of the EV8) all done in hardware would make
these features run at least an order of magnitude faster than if the OS
was doing them.  The earlier versions just had features like branch
prediction and out of order execution (not that they were trivial
capabilities).

It really is kind of sad to know that this will be the last "Alpha"
processor.




