Rodolfo Segleau wrote:

>each "dynamic duo" board has two cpus. each cpu has two zboxes (0 & 1).
>each zbox has a bank of RIMMs, consisiting of 4 RIMM slots for actual
>memory and a 5th slot for parity. the parity is an optional thing, but
>if a RIMM is installed, it must be of the same size as the others in
>the board (in other words, if you have 2GB of memory in 512MB RIMMs,
>the parity RIMM has to be 512MB).
>
>since each cpu has the zbox locally, if any of the cpus have to access
>memory that belongs to a remote processor, the request gets routed
>through the NWSE ports (if it's a gs1280 Mx) or through NS if it's an
>ES47/80. the lag time is minimal, about 300ms from the middle of the
>torus mesh to the exteriors.

Did you mean 300 milliseconds?  That kind of propagation delay seems
really long.  In an 8*8 grid arranged as a torus, I see the minimum
hops between the two furthest nodes is 8.  I hope the delay at each
node is much less than 37.5 milliseconds, or even 37.5 microseconds,
especially if the processor is running at 1+ GHz.

I just realized that this kind of detail is not provided in the
es47_es80_gs1280_ts document.

eyc




