Even the most ardent..the most disciplined among us, at some point in time
has to admit that Alpha was and is THE compute engine. This is the
engineering in either CPU clock or CPU to memory, and memory to PCI that
others have worked to keep up with for over a decade.

I've suggested over a long period of time (in just a few words rather than
paragraph after paragraph) that if the requirement is a stable hardware
architecture, coupled with outstanding operating systems (not just one),
Alpha is IT and certain costs have to be factored in.

This is not to say I'm comfortable with the margins DEC/Compaq/and now HP
feel are appropriate within the product line. I've actually seen the margin
built into Marvel, and find them to be repulsive, especially given the
margin Compaq and now HP expect their business partners to work with.

What's important though:
- nearly 100% accuracy in the execution of code
- ensuring errors are found/corrected without application interruption (up
time)
- data check pointing
- scaling along applications and in conjunction with other diverse
applications (partitioning)
- pre-failure analysis and system management notification
- lights out and remote system management
- fault tolerance
- disaster preparedness/recovery
- computer room design and management
- system security
- development tools (again - across a number of operating systems)
- consistency across third party applications and databases
- the engineering that goes into Alpha product which eventually finds it's
way 	into Intel based products (sooner or later)

To all those that take a narrow view of what this product (Alpha) has
offered over the last decade, I suggest some consideration be given to what
the actual (bottom line) costs are for "less expensive" solutions. Whether
or not your funding comes from one source or several different "pots", the
overall cost per transaction is the same when the variables are factored
in. Even to those within higher education, I might (ever so humbly)
suggest, that reinventing the wheel brings with it a cost. And by the way,
to those of you within higher education, a great deal of your expense is
already supplemented each year by DEC/Compaq/HP by way of millions in
campus/departmental grants. 

To entertain the notion that CPU/memory/IO starved users will receive a
price/performance breakthrough in the near future is nothing short of a
dream. Bottom line costs are what they are. It's only a matter of how frank
and honest we are about the numbers.

A last thought....Marvel data has been available on our web site for
months. Latency is roughly 140ns to the "nearest" adjacent node (75ns
initial dram access+30ns network overhead+18ns wire delay), then 75ns per
hop in and 8x8 configuration. Pretty impressive considering the bulk in
near-node delay is in the dram, and passing data to from the most remote
node in an 8x8 matrix is less than 4x that of the dram itself. Is anyone
else passing data at less than 75ns per node or per CPU?

Maybe, just maybe what we want (after all) is not the least expensive
solution offered, but a solution that meets the technical requirements, and
in the end satisfies (all) the financials.

Cheers....~m
 

